Sources and honesty.

24
00:01:51.548 --> 00:01:57.585
The following are marked VERIFY and should be checked against current official documents before you rely on them:

25
00:01:57.865 --> 00:02:06.441
India's smart meter programme under the Revamped Distribution Sector Scheme (RDSS) and the AMISP (AMI Service Provider) model.

26
00:02:06.721 --> 00:02:16.407
Standards such as IS 16444 (smart meters) and IS 15959 (DLMS companion spec).

27
00:02:16.687 --> 00:02:25.647
CEA regulations and state electricity regulatory commission (SERC) supply codes, which set rules on prepaid disconnection timing.

28
00:02:25.927 --> 00:02:28.167
Everything else is a reference design.

29
00:02:29.517 --> 00:02:30.370
Part 1.

30
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.186
The System in One Page.

31
00:02:33.436 --> 00:02:35.185
What a smart meter platform does.

32
00:02:35.465 --> 00:02:38.559
A traditional meter is read by a person once a month.

33
00:02:38.839 --> 00:02:56.865
A smart meter: records energy every 15 or 30 minutes (load profile);. records voltage, current, power factor and events (tamper, power fail, cover open);. sends all of this to the utility automatically;. can be disconnected or reconnected remotely through a built-in relay;.

34
00:02:58.215 --> 00:03:07.154
supports prepaid mode, where the consumer recharges like a mobile SIM and supply is cut when the balance runs out (with rules about when).

35
00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:17.546
For the utility this means: accurate billing without meter readers;. fewer billing disputes;. faster detection of outages and theft;. better collections through prepaid;.

36
00:03:18.896 --> 00:03:25.765
energy accounting per transformer and feeder to find AT and C losses (aggregate technical and commercial losses).

37
00:03:27.115 --> 00:03:28.203
Main systems.

38
00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:29.827
Here is a quick table:

39
00:03:30.107 --> 00:03:31.622
System, and What it does.

40
00:03:31.902 --> 00:03:32.969
Smart meters:

41
00:03:33.249 --> 00:03:35.638
Measure and store interval data and events.

42
00:03:35.918 --> 00:03:37.795
Relay for connect or disconnect.

43
00:03:38.075 --> 00:03:39.547
Communication module.

44
00:03:39.827 --> 00:03:40.638
Network:

45
00:03:40.918 --> 00:03:49.963
RF mesh with data concentrator units (DCUs)/gateways, or direct cellular (NB-IoT or 4G), or PLC.

46
00:03:50.243 --> 00:03:52.035
Head-End System (HES):

47
00:03:52.315 --> 00:04:01.617
Manages meter communications: schedules reads, sends commands (disconnect, tariff update, time sync), receives pushed events, manages keys.

48
00:04:01.897 --> 00:04:04.222
Meter Data Management (MDM):

49
00:04:04.502 --> 00:04:12.630
Stores and validates interval data, estimates gaps, aggregates for billing, maintains meter, consumer, transformer relationships.

50
00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.233
Billing and CIS:

51
00:04:14.513 --> 00:04:19.057
Customer information system: accounts, tariffs, postpaid bills, payments.

52
00:04:20.407 --> 00:04:21.473
Prepaid engine:

53
00:04:21.753 --> 00:04:27.087
Real-time balance, deductions, low-balance alerts, disconnect or reconnect decisions.

54
00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.647
Recharge channels:

55
00:04:28.927 --> 00:04:31.572
App, UPI, wallets, BBPS, counters.

56
00:04:31.852 --> 00:04:33.793
Outage management (OMS):

57
00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:38.383
Detects outages from last gasp events, groups them, dispatches crews.

58
00:04:38.663 --> 00:04:40.433
Network model (GIS):

59
00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:42.847
Feeders, transformers, consumers.

60
00:04:43.127 --> 00:04:45.665
The connectivity used for energy accounting.

61
00:04:45.945 --> 00:04:46.884
Analytics:

62
00:04:47.164 --> 00:04:51.580
Losses, theft, forecasting, consumer insights, regulatory reports.

63
00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.076
Device management:

64
00:04:53.356 --> 00:04:57.559
Firmware versions, OTA campaigns, configuration, inventory.

65
00:04:58.909 --> 00:04:59.911
Consumer app:

66
00:05:00.191 --> 00:05:03.498
Daily usage, balance, recharge, alerts, complaints.

67
00:05:04.848 --> 00:05:07.003
Scale for this design (assumptions).

68
00:05:07.283 --> 00:05:08.627
Here is a quick table:

69
00:05:08.907 --> 00:05:10.229
Quantity, and Value.

70
00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:11.341
Meters:

71
00:05:11.621 --> 00:05:22.309
25M (mostly single-phase residential); 50k DCUs; 200k distribution transformers (DT meters); 5k feeder meters.

72
00:05:22.589 --> 00:05:23.635
Interval data:

73
00:05:23.915 --> 00:05:33.643
15-minute load profile, then 96 intervals per day per meter, several channels each (kWh import or export, voltage, current).

74
00:05:33.923 --> 00:05:35.011
Daily readings:

75
00:05:35.291 --> 00:05:40.987
25M times 96 times ~4 channels about 10B values per day.

76
00:05:41.267 --> 00:05:42.781
Daily billing snapshots:

77
00:05:43.061 --> 00:05:45.259
25M midnight registers.

78
00:05:45.539 --> 00:05:46.328
Events:

79
00:05:46.608 --> 00:05:53.200
Power fail or restore, tamper, relay operations: tens of millions per day, with bursts during storms.

80
00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:58.771
Prepaid recharges: ~1M per day, with peaks around month start and evenings.

81
00:06:00.121 --> 00:06:00.953
Commands:

82
00:06:01.233 --> 00:06:06.779
Disconnect or reconnect: hundreds of thousands a day at peak (VERIFY rules on timing).

83
00:06:08.129 --> 00:06:09.516
Estimation highlights:

84
00:06:09.796 --> 00:06:10.841
Ingest rate.

85
00:06:11.121 --> 00:06:16.348
10B values per day is about 115k values per second on average.

86
00:06:16.628 --> 00:06:23.775
Reads are scheduled in batches (for example, hourly or four times a day), so peaks are 5 to 10 times higher.

87
00:06:24.055 --> 00:06:24.929
Storage.

88
00:06:25.209 --> 00:06:35.001
At ~12 bytes per compressed value, the raw volume is ~120 GB per day, or ~44 TB per year before replication.

89
00:06:35.281 --> 00:06:39.889
Time-series compression (delta-of-delta, Gorilla-style) reduces this further.

90
00:06:41.239 --> 00:06:46.722
Retain detailed data for several years, because regulators often require it (VERIFY).

91
00:06:47.002 --> 00:06:48.026
Storm events.

92
00:06:48.306 --> 00:06:53.661
When a feeder trips, every meter on it sends a last gasp power-fail event at the same instant:

93
00:06:53.941 --> 00:06:56.415
10k events in a second from one feeder.

94
00:06:56.695 --> 00:07:00.066
A regional storm can produce millions of events in minutes.

95
00:07:00.346 --> 00:07:06.341
The event pipeline must absorb these bursts and correlate them rather than create a million outage tickets.

96
00:07:07.691 --> 00:07:09.013
Network constraints.

97
00:07:09.293 --> 00:07:15.245
RF mesh bandwidth is small, often tens of kbps shared across a DCU's meters.

98
00:07:15.525 --> 00:07:21.328
Reading 1,000 meters' full load profile through one DCU must be scheduled carefully.

99
00:07:21.608 --> 00:07:23.699
You cannot poll everything every minute.

100
00:07:25.049 --> 00:07:25.923
Part 2.

101
00:07:26.203 --> 00:07:27.163
Requirements.

102
00:07:28.413 --> 00:07:29.267
Functional.

103
00:07:29.547 --> 00:07:30.976
Meter data collection.

104
00:07:31.256 --> 00:07:33.837
Daily billing profile (midnight registers).

105
00:07:34.117 --> 00:07:36.443
Load profile (15 or 30 min).

106
00:07:36.723 --> 00:07:38.877
Instantaneous parameters on demand.

107
00:07:39.157 --> 00:07:42.229
Events pushed in near real time (power fail, tamper).

108
00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:43.363
Commands.

109
00:07:43.643 --> 00:07:45.605
Remote disconnect or reconnect.

110
00:07:46.955 --> 00:07:47.873
Time sync.

111
00:07:48.153 --> 00:07:50.286
Tariff or TOD schedule updates.

112
00:07:50.566 --> 00:07:51.846
Load limit settings.

113
00:07:52.126 --> 00:07:54.451
Mode change (prepaid or postpaid).

114
00:07:54.731 --> 00:07:55.926
Firmware updates.

115
00:07:56.206 --> 00:07:56.910
VEE.

116
00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:02.566
Validate readings, estimate missing or invalid data, allow audited manual edits.

117
00:08:02.846 --> 00:08:03.571
Billing.

118
00:08:04.921 --> 00:08:10.596
Postpaid bills with slab and time-of-day tariffs, fixed charges, taxes and subsidies.

119
00:08:10.876 --> 00:08:14.204
Net metering for rooftop solar (import or export).

120
00:08:14.484 --> 00:08:15.359
Prepaid.

121
00:08:15.639 --> 00:08:18.796
Recharge through many channels, credited in real time.

122
00:08:19.076 --> 00:08:20.292
Daily deductions.

123
00:08:20.572 --> 00:08:22.727
Balance and days-remaining projection.

124
00:08:23.007 --> 00:08:24.308
Low-balance alerts.

125
00:08:25.658 --> 00:08:34.362
Disconnect after grace, under regulatory rules (no disconnection at night or on holidays, and emergency credit where mandated; VERIFY).

126
00:08:34.642 --> 00:08:37.351
Automatic reconnect on recharge within minutes.

127
00:08:37.631 --> 00:08:38.506
Outages.

128
00:08:38.786 --> 00:08:50.285
Detect outages from meter events, identify the affected transformer or feeder, notify consumers, track restoration and compute reliability indices (SAIFI or SAIDI).

129
00:08:50.565 --> 00:08:51.802
Energy accounting.

130
00:08:52.082 --> 00:09:00.743
Compare the energy input at the feeder and DT against the sum of consumer meters, to compute losses by area and flag theft hotspots.

131
00:09:02.093 --> 00:09:03.096
Consumer app.

132
00:09:03.376 --> 00:09:07.173
Usage graphs, bill and balance, recharge, alerts, complaints.

133
00:09:07.453 --> 00:09:08.691
Device management.

134
00:09:08.971 --> 00:09:16.907
Inventory, installation workflows (meter swap, mapping to consumer and DT), FOTA campaigns, configuration audits.

135
00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:19.366
Non-functional.

136
00:09:19.646 --> 00:09:20.990
Here is a quick table:

137
00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:22.635
Attribute, and Target.

138
00:09:22.915 --> 00:09:35.609
Data completeness: for example, 98 to 99% of meters with daily data delivered within 24 h, and load profile similar (SLA-driven in AMISP contracts; VERIFY).

139
00:09:35.889 --> 00:09:36.806
Latency:

140
00:09:37.086 --> 00:09:38.921
Events ingested in 1 min.

141
00:09:39.201 --> 00:09:42.443
Reconnect command executed within minutes of a recharge.

142
00:09:42.723 --> 00:09:43.641
Correctness:

143
00:09:43.921 --> 00:09:47.846
Billing-grade accuracy with a full audit of estimates and edits.

144
00:09:48.126 --> 00:09:48.979
Safety:

145
00:09:49.259 --> 00:09:51.329
Disconnects only when policy allows.

146
00:09:51.609 --> 00:09:53.742
Never mass-disconnect because of a bug.

147
00:09:54.022 --> 00:09:55.025
Security:

148
00:09:55.305 --> 00:10:00.510
Per-meter keys, encrypted and authenticated DLMS, no unauthorised commands.

149
00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:01.835
Scalability:

150
00:10:02.115 --> 00:10:04.569
25M+ meters and growing.

151
00:10:04.849 --> 00:10:06.193
Bursts during storms.

152
00:10:07.543 --> 00:10:08.801
Interoperability:

153
00:10:09.081 --> 00:10:12.623
Multiple meter makes through standard DLMS or COSEM.

154
00:10:12.903 --> 00:10:16.401
HES, MDM integration through standard interfaces.

155
00:10:16.681 --> 00:10:17.620
Retention:

156
00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:22.124
Years of interval data for disputes and regulatory audits (VERIFY).

157
00:10:23.474 --> 00:10:24.370
Part 3.

158
00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:26.165
High-Level Architecture.

159
00:10:27.415 --> 00:10:28.759
Here is the diagram.

160
00:10:32.609 --> 00:10:33.654
Key decisions.

161
00:10:35.004 --> 00:10:36.348
Here is a quick table:

162
00:10:36.628 --> 00:10:38.399
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

163
00:10:38.679 --> 00:10:39.639
Protocol.

164
00:10:39.919 --> 00:10:40.751
Choice:

165
00:10:41.031 --> 00:10:51.313
DLMS or COSEM (IEC 62056) with the national companion spec (IS 15959; VERIFY).

166
00:10:51.593 --> 00:10:52.276
Why:

167
00:10:52.556 --> 00:10:54.625
Multi-vendor interoperability.

168
00:10:54.905 --> 00:10:55.716
Network.

169
00:10:55.996 --> 00:10:56.828
Choice:

170
00:10:57.108 --> 00:10:57.855
Mixed:

171
00:10:58.135 --> 00:11:04.513
RF mesh in dense urban areas, cellular in sparse or rural areas and for DT meters.

172
00:11:04.793 --> 00:11:05.476
Why:

173
00:11:05.756 --> 00:11:07.207
Coverage versus cost.

174
00:11:07.487 --> 00:11:09.556
HES versus MDM split.

175
00:11:09.836 --> 00:11:10.668
Choice:

176
00:11:10.948 --> 00:11:12.804
HES owns communication.

177
00:11:13.084 --> 00:11:14.705
MDM owns data truth.

178
00:11:14.985 --> 00:11:15.668
Why:

179
00:11:15.948 --> 00:11:17.655
Swap vendors independently.

180
00:11:17.935 --> 00:11:18.745
Ingest.

181
00:11:19.025 --> 00:11:19.857
Choice:

182
00:11:20.137 --> 00:11:22.015
HES publishes to Kafka.

183
00:11:22.295 --> 00:11:25.900
Consumers (MDM, OMS, prepaid) read independently.

184
00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:26.863
Why:

185
00:11:27.143 --> 00:11:28.380
Decoupling, replay.

186
00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:29.535
Storage.

187
00:11:29.815 --> 00:11:30.647
Choice:

188
00:11:30.927 --> 00:11:36.473
Time-series DB or a columnar store for interval data, relational for master data.

189
00:11:36.753 --> 00:11:37.436
Why:

190
00:11:37.716 --> 00:11:39.167
Fit the access patterns.

191
00:11:40.517 --> 00:11:41.391
Prepaid.

192
00:11:41.671 --> 00:11:42.503
Choice:

193
00:11:42.783 --> 00:11:48.586
Utility-side balance (a smart prepaid meter reporting consumption), not balance in the meter.

194
00:11:48.866 --> 00:11:49.549
Why:

195
00:11:49.829 --> 00:11:52.218
Easy tariff changes, central control.

196
00:11:52.498 --> 00:11:53.351
Commands.

197
00:11:53.631 --> 00:11:54.463
Choice:

198
00:11:54.743 --> 00:11:58.391
An idempotent, rate-limited command queue with policy checks.

199
00:11:58.671 --> 00:11:59.354
Why:

200
00:11:59.634 --> 00:12:00.807
Safety at scale.

201
00:12:02.157 --> 00:12:03.075
Part 4.

202
00:12:03.355 --> 00:12:05.253
How It Works, End-to-End Journeys.

203
00:12:06.503 --> 00:12:08.573
Journey 1: a day of meter data.

204
00:12:08.853 --> 00:12:09.962
Inside the meter.

205
00:12:10.242 --> 00:12:19.138
Every 15 minutes it records the block load profile: kWh import, kWh export, average voltage, average current.

206
00:12:19.418 --> 00:12:23.834
The data is stored in the meter's memory, which holds days to weeks of intervals.

207
00:12:24.114 --> 00:12:27.058
At midnight it snapshots the daily billing registers.

208
00:12:27.338 --> 00:12:28.725
Communication paths.

209
00:12:29.005 --> 00:12:32.546
RF mesh: the meter's radio forms a mesh with neighbours.

210
00:12:32.826 --> 00:12:38.479
Data hops to a DCU on a pole, and the DCU backhauls over 4G to the HES.

211
00:12:38.759 --> 00:12:45.735
Cellular: an NB-IoT or 4G module talks to the HES directly (APN or VPN).

212
00:12:46.015 --> 00:12:48.426
Read schedule (§6.8).

213
00:12:49.776 --> 00:12:53.552
The HES scheduler plans reads to fit network capacity.

214
00:12:53.832 --> 00:12:56.691
Daily profiles go out after midnight in waves.

215
00:12:56.971 --> 00:13:00.299
Load profile is read every 4 to 8 hours in batches.

216
00:13:00.579 --> 00:13:06.040
Some meters are configured to push data at set times, with jitter so they don't all send at once.

217
00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:07.856
The HES talks DLMS.

218
00:13:08.136 --> 00:13:12.189
It opens an association with the meter using security suite keys.

219
00:13:13.539 --> 00:13:25.315
It reads the COSEM objects (OBIS codes identify each quantity, for example, 1.0.1.8.0.255 for active import energy).

220
00:13:25.595 --> 00:13:28.603
It receives encrypted and authenticated responses.

221
00:13:28.883 --> 00:13:38.398
The HES publishes raw readings to Kafka (meter.reads.raw) with meter ID, OBIS code, timestamp, value, quality flags and read time.

222
00:13:38.678 --> 00:13:41.217
MDM VEE (§6.1).

223
00:13:41.497 --> 00:13:44.526
The MDM consumes readings and validates them:

224
00:13:44.806 --> 00:13:54.257
Checks: timestamps in range, no negative consumption on cumulative registers, spike checks, interval sum versus register delta, and more.

225
00:13:55.607 --> 00:13:59.916
Gaps are estimated (§6.2) and flagged as estimated.

226
00:14:00.196 --> 00:14:03.268
Validated data is written to the time-series store.

227
00:14:03.548 --> 00:14:06.151
Daily aggregates feed billing and prepaid.

228
00:14:06.431 --> 00:14:07.561
Missing meters.

229
00:14:07.841 --> 00:14:16.225
Meters not read by the target time get retries, and are reported in communication-health dashboards (by DCU, area and vendor).

230
00:14:16.505 --> 00:14:18.703
Persistent failures create field tickets.

231
00:14:18.983 --> 00:14:20.007
Consumer view.

232
00:14:20.287 --> 00:14:25.684
The next morning the app shows yesterday's usage by hour, plus a comparison with similar homes.

233
00:14:27.034 --> 00:14:29.935
Journey 2: a prepaid recharge and reconnection.

234
00:14:30.215 --> 00:14:31.218
Disconnection.

235
00:14:31.498 --> 00:14:34.229
Priya's balance fell below zero yesterday evening.

236
00:14:34.509 --> 00:14:39.885
Under the regulatory rules the system did not disconnect at night (VERIFY the exact rule).

237
00:14:40.165 --> 00:14:48.079
It disconnected this morning at 10:00 after a grace period, and sent her SMS and app alerts before and after.

238
00:14:48.359 --> 00:14:49.319
Recharge.

239
00:14:49.599 --> 00:14:53.439
Priya recharges 500 rupeesby UPI in the app.

240
00:14:53.719 --> 00:15:00.567
The payment gateway confirms, and the prepaid engine credits her balance idempotently, using the payment reference.

241
00:15:01.917 --> 00:15:03.176
Reconnect decision.

242
00:15:03.456 --> 00:15:06.443
The balance is now above the reconnection threshold.

243
00:15:06.723 --> 00:15:12.184
The engine creates a reconnect command for her meter, with the reason recharge and a reference.

244
00:15:12.464 --> 00:15:15.088
Command manager (§5.5).

245
00:15:15.368 --> 00:15:21.192
The command passes policy checks: meter ID valid, not flagged for tamper, reconnect allowed.

246
00:15:21.472 --> 00:15:23.264
It is queued with high priority.

247
00:15:23.544 --> 00:15:28.301
The HES sends a DLMS action to the disconnect-control object.

248
00:15:28.581 --> 00:15:31.525
The meter's relay closes and power is restored.

249
00:15:31.805 --> 00:15:37.757
The meter confirms the relay status, and the HES publishes RelayStatusChanged(connected).

250
00:15:39.107 --> 00:15:40.195
Notification.

251
00:15:40.475 --> 00:15:46.043
The engine marks the command complete and sends Power reconnected to Priya, typically within minutes.

252
00:15:46.323 --> 00:15:47.518
Failure handling.

253
00:15:47.798 --> 00:15:53.985
If the meter is unreachable (for example, the RF mesh is weak), the command retries with backoff.

254
00:15:54.265 --> 00:15:56.931
After a timeout it escalates to a field visit.

255
00:15:57.211 --> 00:16:00.859
Priya sees Reconnection in progress and can call the helpline.

256
00:16:02.209 --> 00:16:04.449
Journey 3: daily prepaid deduction.

257
00:16:04.729 --> 00:16:05.647
Consumption.

258
00:16:05.927 --> 00:16:13.735
After midnight the MDM publishes validated daily consumption for each prepaid meter, broken down by time-of-day slot.

259
00:16:14.015 --> 00:16:15.999
Rating (§6.3).

260
00:16:16.279 --> 00:16:32.471
The prepaid engine calculates the charges: energy charges by slab and TOD rate;. fixed charges pro-rated per day;. electricity duty and taxes;. subsidies (for example, free units for eligible consumers under state schemes; VERIFY).

261
00:16:33.821 --> 00:16:37.618
It then deducts the total from the balance and records ledger entries.

262
00:16:37.898 --> 00:16:40.586
Balance projection (§6.4).

263
00:16:40.866 --> 00:16:43.981
Days remaining are estimated from recent consumption.

264
00:16:44.261 --> 00:16:47.781
If fewer than N days remain, a low-balance alert goes out.

265
00:16:48.061 --> 00:16:49.255
Negative balance.

266
00:16:49.535 --> 00:17:02.207
If the balance goes negative, the case enters the disconnect workflow: check the policy windows (time of day, holidays, emergency credit);. then schedule a disconnect command in the next allowed window.

267
00:17:03.557 --> 00:17:04.965
Near real-time mode.

268
00:17:05.245 --> 00:17:12.200
Some utilities deduct more frequently (hourly) using load profile data, which gives faster balance feedback.

269
00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:16.299
Daily finalisation reconciles against the validated data.

270
00:17:17.649 --> 00:17:20.209
Journey 4: a transformer trips in a storm.

271
00:17:20.489 --> 00:17:21.470
Last gasp.

272
00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:25.505
A distribution transformer's fuse blows at 19:42.

273
00:17:25.785 --> 00:17:31.097
Its 300 consumer meters lose power, and each sends a last gasp power-fail event.

274
00:17:31.377 --> 00:17:36.945
This works on cellular meters with a supercapacitor, and on RF meters when the mesh survives.

275
00:17:37.225 --> 00:17:40.297
Some events are lost because the mesh nodes are down too.

276
00:17:40.577 --> 00:17:41.387
Ingest.

277
00:17:41.667 --> 00:17:47.470
The HES receives thousands of events within seconds and publishes them to meter.events.

278
00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:50.609
OMS correlation (§6.5).

279
00:17:51.959 --> 00:17:58.956
The OMS groups events by time window and network topology from GIS (the meters on the same DT).

280
00:17:59.236 --> 00:18:06.361
If more than X% of meters under a DT report power-fail within a minute, it infers a DT-level outage.

281
00:18:06.641 --> 00:18:09.735
Meters with no event are inferred as affected too.

282
00:18:10.015 --> 00:18:15.433
If multiple DTs on one feeder fail together, it escalates to a feeder-level outage.

283
00:18:15.713 --> 00:18:19.660
Individual power-fail events are suppressed into one outage ticket.

284
00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:20.857
Response.

285
00:18:22.207 --> 00:18:24.959
A crew is dispatched with the DT's location.

286
00:18:25.239 --> 00:18:26.818
Consumers get an SMS:

287
00:18:27.098 --> 00:18:32.538
Outage in your area, crew assigned, estimated restoration 21:00.

288
00:18:32.818 --> 00:18:36.253
Call-centre agents see the known outage when customers call.

289
00:18:36.533 --> 00:18:37.599
Restoration.

290
00:18:37.879 --> 00:18:41.143
When power returns, the meters send power-restore events.

291
00:18:41.423 --> 00:18:47.098
The OMS verifies restoration (a ping of a sample of meters) and closes the ticket.

292
00:18:47.378 --> 00:18:54.418
If some meters are still off, that suggests a nested outage (a single service line), and a new ticket is created.

293
00:18:55.768 --> 00:18:57.261
Reliability indices.

294
00:18:57.541 --> 00:19:02.469
Outage durations per consumer feed SAIDI and SAIFI for regulatory reporting.

295
00:19:03.819 --> 00:19:06.230
Journey 5: catching electricity theft.

296
00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:07.598
Energy balance.

297
00:19:07.878 --> 00:19:09.670
Each DT has its own meter.

298
00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:16.777
Every day, energy accounting compares DT output with the sum of its consumers' consumption (after VEE).

299
00:19:17.057 --> 00:19:21.366
It adjusts for expected technical losses (§6.7).

300
00:19:21.646 --> 00:19:25.145
A DT with 25% unexplained loss is flagged.

301
00:19:25.425 --> 00:19:34.278
Consumer-level anomalies under flagged DTs (§6.6): a sudden drop in consumption without a matching seasonal pattern;.

302
00:19:35.628 --> 00:19:47.127
tamper events (cover open, magnetic tamper, neutral disturbance, reverse current);. consumption far below similar homes;. zero consumption while the neighbouring meters show usage.

303
00:19:47.407 --> 00:19:48.815
Scoring and inspection.

304
00:19:49.095 --> 00:19:51.207
A model ranks the suspect consumers.

305
00:19:51.487 --> 00:19:55.604
An inspection team visits with a mobile app, and findings are recorded.

306
00:19:55.884 --> 00:19:56.759
Feedback.

307
00:19:57.039 --> 00:20:01.775
The inspection results (theft found or not) are fed back to retrain the model.

308
00:20:03.125 --> 00:20:06.239
Journey 6: a firmware update to 2 million meters.

309
00:20:06.519 --> 00:20:07.373
The fix.

310
00:20:07.653 --> 00:20:11.322
A meter vendor releases firmware that fixes a clock drift bug.

311
00:20:11.602 --> 00:20:17.127
The utility tests it in the lab, then on a pilot group of 1,000 meters in a test area.

312
00:20:17.407 --> 00:20:20.010
Staged campaign (§6.9).

313
00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:25.303
Waves go 1%, then 5%, then 20%, then 100%.

314
00:20:25.583 --> 00:20:34.735
Each wave has health gates: meters come back online after the update, read success rates stay stable, no increase in errors or tamper events.

315
00:20:35.015 --> 00:20:37.789
Waves are paused automatically if the gates fail.

316
00:20:39.139 --> 00:20:40.013
Bandwidth.

317
00:20:40.293 --> 00:20:45.179
Firmware images (hundreds of KB) are sent over a constrained RF mesh.

318
00:20:45.459 --> 00:20:50.963
Use multicast or broadcast image transfer where supported (DLMS image transfer).

319
00:20:51.243 --> 00:20:53.248
Schedule during low-traffic hours.

320
00:20:53.528 --> 00:20:55.576
Transfer blocks with resume support.

321
00:20:55.856 --> 00:20:56.901
Activation.

322
00:20:57.181 --> 00:21:02.365
The image is verified by signature on the meter, then activated at a scheduled time.

323
00:21:02.645 --> 00:21:07.445
The meter reports its new version, and the device management inventory is updated.

324
00:21:08.795 --> 00:21:09.649
Rollback.

325
00:21:09.929 --> 00:21:14.985
A failed activation falls back to the previous image where the meter supports dual banks.

326
00:21:16.335 --> 00:21:17.295
Part 5.

327
00:21:17.575 --> 00:21:19.111
Core Subsystems in Depth.

328
00:21:20.361 --> 00:21:21.491
Head-End System.

329
00:21:21.771 --> 00:21:29.579
Communication servers keep DLMS sessions over TCP or UDP (for cellular), or via the DCUs.

330
00:21:29.859 --> 00:21:33.699
They scale horizontally, and meters are partitioned across servers.

331
00:21:33.979 --> 00:21:44.177
The scheduler holds job queues per network segment (DCU, cell), respecting capacity, priorities (commands above reads), time windows and retries.

332
00:21:44.457 --> 00:21:45.459
Security:

333
00:21:46.809 --> 00:22:06.137
DLMS security suites for authentication and encryption, with per-meter keys (global unicast encryption key, authentication key, master key);. keys stored in an HSM and rotated;. meters are provisioned at the factory with keys shared through a secure key exchange (VERIFY standard practices).

334
00:22:06.417 --> 00:22:13.628
Multi-vendor adapters cover the vendor quirks in DLMS implementations and conformance to the companion spec.

335
00:22:13.908 --> 00:22:18.239
Outputs go to Kafka: raw reads, events and command acknowledgements.

336
00:22:19.589 --> 00:22:20.506
MDM.

337
00:22:20.786 --> 00:22:35.165
Master data: meters, consumers, service points, DT and feeder relationships (from GIS or CIS), meter swaps with effective dates, and multipliers (CT or PT ratios for large consumers).

338
00:22:35.445 --> 00:22:43.957
VEE engine: a rules pipeline per reading type (§6.1 to 6.2), with audit of every estimate and edit.

339
00:22:44.237 --> 00:22:46.967
Aggregation: daily and monthly consumption;.

340
00:22:47.247 --> 00:22:48.442
TOD buckets;.

341
00:22:49.792 --> 00:22:56.021
DT and feeder aggregates for energy accounting;. peak demand (max demand in a billing period).

342
00:22:56.301 --> 00:23:06.179
Interfaces: standard messages (IEC 61968 to 9 CIM-based; VERIFY) towards the HES and billing.

343
00:23:07.529 --> 00:23:08.915
Time-series storage.

344
00:23:09.195 --> 00:23:15.745
Shape of the data: key = (meter ID, channel), value = (timestamp, value, quality flag).

345
00:23:16.025 --> 00:23:25.838
Access patterns: write in batches after reads;. read one meter's range (app, disputes);. aggregate across millions of meters for a time range (analytics).

346
00:23:26.118 --> 00:23:26.993
Options:

347
00:23:28.343 --> 00:23:41.761
a time-series DB (TimescaleDB, InfluxDB, Cassandra with time buckets) for per-meter queries;. columnar lakehouse tables (Parquet or Iceberg, partitioned by date and region) for analytics.

348
00:23:42.041 --> 00:23:47.823
Many utilities use both: hot recent data in the TSDB, and everything in the lakehouse.

349
00:23:48.103 --> 00:23:53.884
Corrections: re-estimation or late reads replace values, with a version and quality flag.

350
00:23:54.164 --> 00:23:57.065
Analytics must use the latest validated version.

351
00:23:58.415 --> 00:23:59.503
Prepaid engine.

352
00:23:59.783 --> 00:24:06.653
Ledger per consumer account: recharges (credits), deductions (debits), adjustments, and emergency credit.

353
00:24:06.933 --> 00:24:10.687
Double-entry, idempotent by reference (I11 patterns).

354
00:24:10.967 --> 00:24:16.855
Balance state: current balance;. last deduction time;. projected days;. disconnect status;.

355
00:24:18.205 --> 00:24:20.296
grace and emergency credit flags.

356
00:24:20.576 --> 00:24:38.005
Disconnect policy engine: rules on allowed windows (for example, working hours only, no holidays), notice periods and exemptions (medical priority consumers, where policies exist);. all encoded as configurable rules per state (VERIFY state regulations).

357
00:24:38.285 --> 00:24:41.763
Reconnect SLA: recharge, then reconnect in minutes.

358
00:24:42.043 --> 00:24:44.475
The recharge path must be highly available.

359
00:24:45.825 --> 00:24:47.958
Command manager (safety-critical).

360
00:24:48.238 --> 00:24:52.953
Commands: disconnect, reconnect, load limit, tariff update, time sync, firmware.

361
00:24:53.233 --> 00:24:54.193
Guardrails:

362
00:24:54.473 --> 00:24:56.606
Rate limits per area and in total.

363
00:24:56.886 --> 00:25:03.030
A software bug must not disconnect 1M homes, so there is a global circuit breaker on the disconnect rate.

364
00:25:03.310 --> 00:25:05.465
Two-person approval for bulk commands.

365
00:25:05.745 --> 00:25:09.969
Policy checks per command: the consumer's status, balance, time window.

366
00:25:11.319 --> 00:25:15.116
Idempotency: a command ID ensures repeats do nothing.

367
00:25:15.396 --> 00:25:18.148
Verification of the resulting relay state.

368
00:25:18.428 --> 00:25:22.716
A full audit trail: who or what initiated the command, why, and when.

369
00:25:22.996 --> 00:25:26.089
Priority: reconnects above disconnects above reads.

370
00:25:27.439 --> 00:25:28.677
Outage management.

371
00:25:28.957 --> 00:25:33.181
Event correlation with GIS topology (§6.5).

372
00:25:33.461 --> 00:25:35.466
A crew management and dispatch app.

373
00:25:35.746 --> 00:25:42.103
Integration with SCADA for feeder-level trips at substations, which is more authoritative than meter events.

374
00:25:42.383 --> 00:25:43.919
Consumer communications.

375
00:25:44.199 --> 00:25:46.290
Reliability index computation.

376
00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:48.877
Device management.

377
00:25:49.157 --> 00:25:56.155
Inventory: meter serial, make, model, firmware, communication module, installation details and photos.

378
00:25:56.435 --> 00:26:05.651
Configuration audits: the actual settings read from the meter versus the desired settings (tariff schedule, profile interval, push schedules).

379
00:26:05.931 --> 00:26:07.829
Mismatches are fixed with commands.

380
00:26:08.109 --> 00:26:12.611
FOTA campaigns: staged rollout with health gates (§6.9).

381
00:26:12.891 --> 00:26:19.312
Communication health: per meter and per DCU, including the last successful read and signal quality.

382
00:26:20.662 --> 00:26:21.601
Part 6.

383
00:26:21.881 --> 00:26:22.841
Algorithms.

384
00:26:24.091 --> 00:26:25.797
VEE validation rules.

385
00:26:26.077 --> 00:26:30.408
Timestamp checks: within an expected window, and no future timestamps.

386
00:26:30.688 --> 00:26:34.379
Clock drift beyond a tolerance flags the meter for a time sync.

387
00:26:34.659 --> 00:26:42.765
Cumulative register checks: kWh registers must be non-decreasing unless the meter was replaced (a meter swap event).

388
00:26:43.045 --> 00:26:50.725
Interval versus register consistency: the sum of the day's intervals should equal the midnight register delta, within tolerance.

389
00:26:51.005 --> 00:27:00.008
Spike and high or low limits: consumption above the connected load times time, or zero consumption while the voltage shows supply present.

390
00:27:01.358 --> 00:27:08.782
Pattern checks: usage far outside the consumer's historical distribution, flagged for review rather than auto-corrected.

391
00:27:09.062 --> 00:27:16.337
Event context: intervals overlapping a power failure should show zero consumption, which is valid and not a gap.

392
00:27:17.687 --> 00:27:20.161
Estimation of missing or invalid data.

393
00:27:20.441 --> 00:27:25.113
Short gaps (a few intervals): linear interpolation between neighbouring intervals.

394
00:27:25.393 --> 00:27:32.305
When registers are available, distribute the known register delta across the missing intervals using the typical shape.

395
00:27:32.585 --> 00:27:44.276
Long gaps (days): use historical profile matching: the same weekday and season, scaled to recent consumption;. or use similar consumers (same tariff and area) as a template.

396
00:27:44.556 --> 00:27:53.260
Billing estimates: if no register data exists at the billing date, estimate from the average daily usage, and true up when real data arrives.

397
00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:57.405
Always record the method, the version and a flag.

398
00:27:57.685 --> 00:28:00.949
Estimated data can be replaced by actual data later.

399
00:28:02.299 --> 00:28:04.411
Tariff rating with time-of-day and slabs.

400
00:28:04.691 --> 00:28:24.915
Inputs: consumption split by TOD slots (peak, normal, off-peak), from the load profile;. the tariff: slabs per month (0 to 100 units at rate A, 101 to 200 at B, and so on), TOD surcharges or rebates, fixed charges per kW of sanctioned load, duties, subsidies.

401
00:28:25.195 --> 00:28:34.795
Monthly postpaid rating: apply the slabs to the monthly total, then apply TOD adjustments per slot, then fixed charges, duty and subsidy.

402
00:28:36.145 --> 00:28:41.585
Daily prepaid: slabs are monthly, so the daily deduction allocates slabs incrementally.

403
00:28:41.865 --> 00:28:48.329
Track the units consumed so far this month and charge each day's units at the slab rates they fall into.

404
00:28:48.609 --> 00:28:52.470
This ensures the month's total equals the postpaid calculation.

405
00:28:52.750 --> 00:29:00.174
Net metering: export units offset import, per regulations (net versus gross metering; VERIFY state rules).

406
00:29:01.524 --> 00:29:03.124
Prepaid balance projection.

407
00:29:03.404 --> 00:29:08.865
Daily burn rate comes from an exponentially weighted moving average of recent days' charges.

408
00:29:09.145 --> 00:29:13.945
Add a seasonal adjustment (summer AC load) and weekday or weekend patterns.

409
00:29:14.225 --> 00:29:18.428
Days remaining = balance ÷ projected daily charge.

410
00:29:18.708 --> 00:29:21.993
Alert when it is below 3 days, and again below 1 day.

411
00:29:22.273 --> 00:29:30.913
Emergency credit: if configured, a small credit allows supply during non-disconnect windows, and is recovered on the next recharge.

412
00:29:32.263 --> 00:29:33.821
Outage inference from events.

413
00:29:34.101 --> 00:29:42.335
Grouping: cluster power-fail events by time (for example, 60 s windows) and by topology node (DT, feeder section).

414
00:29:42.615 --> 00:29:55.117
Upstream inference: if the fraction of meters under a node reporting a failure exceeds a threshold, infer that the node is out;. recurse upward: if multiple child nodes are out, infer the parent.

415
00:29:55.397 --> 00:29:58.085
This is similar to fault localisation in trees.

416
00:29:59.435 --> 00:30:05.067
Missing events: the mesh may be down, so use pings to sample meters and confirm their status.

417
00:30:05.347 --> 00:30:08.269
Restoration: power-restore events clear nodes.

418
00:30:08.549 --> 00:30:12.219
Nested outages are detected when some meters remain off.

419
00:30:12.499 --> 00:30:18.557
Noise: individual meter failures (a tripped home MCB) don't escalate without a threshold.

420
00:30:18.837 --> 00:30:23.467
Momentary interruptions (less than 5 minutes) are counted separately (MAIFI).

421
00:30:24.817 --> 00:30:26.609
Theft and anomaly detection.

422
00:30:26.889 --> 00:30:46.067
Features per consumer: consumption trend breaks (change-point detection);. the ratio to peers' consumption (same tariff, area, sanctioned load);. tamper event counts;. neutral current versus phase current imbalance;. reverse energy;. load profile shape anomalies (flat night usage, for example);.

423
00:30:47.417 --> 00:30:50.041
zero consumption while voltage is present.

424
00:30:50.321 --> 00:31:03.591
Models: rules for strong signals (magnet tamper);. isolation forest or autoencoders for unsupervised anomaly scoring;. supervised gradient-boosted models trained on inspection outcomes.

425
00:31:03.871 --> 00:31:09.631
Prioritisation: combine the consumer score with DT-level loss from the energy balance.

426
00:31:09.911 --> 00:31:14.092
Inspection capacity is limited, so rank by expected recovery.

427
00:31:15.442 --> 00:31:17.703
Energy accounting and technical loss.

428
00:31:17.983 --> 00:31:19.711
For each DT and each day:

429
00:31:19.991 --> 00:31:28.610
Loss = DT output energy minus Σ consumer energy (after VEE, with consistent time windows and multipliers).

430
00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:33.135
Technical loss estimate = transformer losses plus line losses.

431
00:31:33.415 --> 00:31:41.245
Estimate it with a load-flow approximation (I²R using the current profiles) or typical percentages by network type.

432
00:31:41.525 --> 00:31:44.447
Commercial loss about loss minus technical loss.

433
00:31:44.727 --> 00:31:46.455
Data quality prerequisites:

434
00:31:47.805 --> 00:31:57.277
correct consumer-to-DT mapping (a frequent source of false alarms, since GIS mapping errors are common);. complete readings;. meter swaps handled.

435
00:31:57.557 --> 00:32:10.016
Detect mapping errors with voltage correlation: meters on the same DT show correlated voltage profiles, so a meter whose voltage correlates better with another DT is probably mis-mapped.

436
00:32:11.366 --> 00:32:13.670
Read scheduling under network constraints.

437
00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:18.238
Capacity model: each DCU or cell sector has a throughput budget.

438
00:32:18.518 --> 00:32:25.793
Each read job has a size (daily profile is small; load profile for 8 hours is medium; firmware is large).

439
00:32:26.073 --> 00:32:36.910
Scheduling: a bin-packing and priority scheduler over time slots;. randomised jitter for pushes, so meters don't synchronise;. failed reads retry later with backoff.

440
00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:46.793
Adaptive: meters with poor links are read less often and in larger batches, and meters that repeatedly fail are flagged for field checks.

441
00:32:48.143 --> 00:32:49.786
Staged OTA rollout.

442
00:32:50.066 --> 00:32:51.410
Waves with health gates.

443
00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:59.797
Each gate checks metrics against a control group: reconnect rate after reboot;. read success;. error events;. clock accuracy.

444
00:33:00.077 --> 00:33:06.925
Automatic pause if any metric degrades beyond thresholds, with statistical tests to avoid noise-driven pauses.

445
00:33:08.275 --> 00:33:15.464
Diversity: include different vendors, hardware revisions and network types in the early waves, to surface issues early.

446
00:33:16.814 --> 00:33:19.182
Load forecasting and demand response.

447
00:33:19.462 --> 00:33:37.659
Short-term load forecasting per feeder, DT and system: inputs are historical load, weather (temperature, humidity), calendar (holidays, festivals) and special events;. models are gradient boosting or deep sequence models;. forecast horizons run from 15 minutes to a day ahead.

448
00:33:37.939 --> 00:33:40.777
Uses: power purchase planning (day-ahead markets);.

449
00:33:41.057 --> 00:33:42.657
DT overload alerts;.

450
00:33:44.007 --> 00:33:45.991
planning for EV charging growth.

451
00:33:46.271 --> 00:34:01.673
Demand response: send TOD price signals, or ask enrolled consumers to reduce load;. load limiting via meter settings in emergencies (policy);. measure the response against a baseline (for example, the average of similar days).

452
00:34:03.023 --> 00:34:03.962
Part 7.

453
00:34:04.242 --> 00:34:05.565
Data Architecture.

454
00:34:06.815 --> 00:34:08.201
Operational stores.

455
00:34:08.481 --> 00:34:09.825
Here is a quick table:

456
00:34:10.105 --> 00:34:12.281
Data, and Store (reference), and Notes.

457
00:34:12.561 --> 00:34:13.927
Raw reads and events.

458
00:34:14.207 --> 00:34:15.444
Store (reference):

459
00:34:15.724 --> 00:34:19.073
Kafka (retained for days) + raw zone in the lakehouse.

460
00:34:19.353 --> 00:34:20.164
Notes:

461
00:34:20.444 --> 00:34:21.980
Replay for re-processing.

462
00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:23.860
Validated interval data.

