645
00:41:41.772 --> 00:41:43.116
Interview Q and A.

646
00:41:44.366 --> 00:41:45.198
Beginner.

647
00:41:45.478 --> 00:41:46.502
Question 1.

648
00:41:46.782 --> 00:41:50.579
What are the HES and the MDM in a smart metering system?

649
00:41:50.859 --> 00:41:52.225
Thirty-second answer:

650
00:41:52.505 --> 00:42:10.254
The Head-End System talks to meters over the network (DLMS), schedules reads, sends commands and receives events; the Meter Data Management system validates, estimates and stores the data, maintains meter-consumer-network relationships, and feeds billing, prepaid and analytics.

651
00:42:11.604 --> 00:42:12.607
Question 2.

652
00:42:12.887 --> 00:42:16.236
How does a prepaid smart meter reconnect after a recharge?

653
00:42:16.516 --> 00:42:17.881
Thirty-second answer:

654
00:42:18.161 --> 00:42:36.316
The payment credits the balance idempotently, the prepaid engine sees the balance above the threshold and issues a reconnect command, the command manager checks policy and sends a DLMS action via the HES, the meter's relay closes and confirms, and the consumer is notified.

655
00:42:37.666 --> 00:42:38.711
Question 3.

656
00:42:38.991 --> 00:42:41.167
Why validate meter data before billing?

657
00:42:41.447 --> 00:42:42.813
Thirty-second answer:

658
00:42:43.093 --> 00:42:56.447
Reads can be missing, duplicated, have wrong timestamps, spikes or register resets from meter swaps; billing on bad data creates disputes, so VEE checks, estimates gaps with flags, and audits edits.

659
00:42:57.797 --> 00:42:58.864
Question 4.

660
00:42:59.144 --> 00:43:02.877
How much data do 25 million smart meters generate?

661
00:43:03.157 --> 00:43:04.523
Thirty-second answer:

662
00:43:04.803 --> 00:43:22.317
96 intervals a day times a few channels times 25M about 10 billion values a day, around 100k+ values per second on average with big batch peaks, tens of TB a year before compression, so time-series compression and partitioned storage matter.

663
00:43:23.667 --> 00:43:24.670
Intermediate.

664
00:43:24.950 --> 00:43:26.059
Question 5.

665
00:43:26.339 --> 00:43:30.841
How do you detect outages from smart meters without creating a million tickets?

666
00:43:31.121 --> 00:43:32.486
Thirty-second answer:

667
00:43:32.766 --> 00:43:52.798
Ingest last-gasp events into Kafka, group them by time window and network topology from GIS, infer DT- or feeder-level outages when a threshold fraction of child meters fails, confirm with pings and SCADA, create one outage ticket, and close it on restore events, detecting nested outages.

668
00:43:54.148 --> 00:43:55.257
Question 6.

669
00:43:55.537 --> 00:43:57.841
How would you estimate missing interval data?

670
00:43:58.121 --> 00:43:59.487
Thirty-second answer:

671
00:43:59.767 --> 00:44:17.068
Short gaps: interpolate or distribute the known register delta across missing intervals using a typical shape; long gaps: scale historical same-weekday profiles or peer templates; always flag estimates with method and version and replace with actuals when they arrive.

672
00:44:18.418 --> 00:44:19.506
Question 7.

673
00:44:19.786 --> 00:44:23.050
How do you rate slab tariffs daily for prepaid consumers?

674
00:44:23.330 --> 00:44:24.695
Thirty-second answer:

675
00:44:24.975 --> 00:44:38.351
Track month-to-date units, and charge each day's units at the slab rates they fall into incrementally, plus TOD adjustments and pro-rated fixed charges, so the month's deductions equal the postpaid calculation.

676
00:44:39.701 --> 00:44:40.725
Question 8.

677
00:44:41.005 --> 00:44:44.248
How do you schedule reads over a constrained RF mesh?

678
00:44:44.528 --> 00:44:45.893
Thirty-second answer:

679
00:44:46.173 --> 00:44:59.485
Model throughput per DCU or cell, prioritise commands over reads, batch load profile reads, spread push schedules with jitter, retry with backoff, and read poor-link meters less often in bigger batches.

680
00:45:00.835 --> 00:45:01.902
Question 9.

681
00:45:02.182 --> 00:45:04.422
What makes remote disconnect safe at scale?

682
00:45:04.702 --> 00:45:06.067
Thirty-second answer:

683
00:45:06.347 --> 00:45:22.219
Policy checks per command (balance, time windows, exemptions), idempotent command IDs, rate limits per area and globally with a circuit breaker, two-person approval for bulk actions, verification of the resulting relay state, and a full audit trail.

684
00:45:23.569 --> 00:45:24.337
Senior.

685
00:45:24.617 --> 00:45:25.663
Question 10.

686
00:45:25.943 --> 00:45:29.356
How would you find electricity theft using smart meter data?

687
00:45:29.636 --> 00:45:31.001
Thirty-second answer:

688
00:45:31.281 --> 00:45:54.257
Compute daily DT energy balances (DT meter minus consumers minus estimated technical loss) to find high-loss areas, then score consumers under them using tamper events, consumption trend breaks, peer comparison and profile anomalies with rules plus unsupervised and supervised models, and prioritise inspections, feeding results back.

689
00:45:55.607 --> 00:45:56.738
Question 11.

690
00:45:57.018 --> 00:46:00.005
How would you roll out firmware to 2 million meters safely?

691
00:46:00.285 --> 00:46:01.650
Thirty-second answer:

692
00:46:01.930 --> 00:46:18.869
Lab test, a pilot, then staged waves with health gates against a control group (reconnects, read success, errors, clock accuracy), automatic pauses, diverse early waves, multicast image transfer off-peak with resume, signed images, and dual-bank rollback.

693
00:46:20.219 --> 00:46:21.307
Question 12.

694
00:46:21.587 --> 00:46:24.339
How do you store and query years of interval data?

695
00:46:24.619 --> 00:46:25.984
Thirty-second answer:

696
00:46:26.264 --> 00:46:40.387
Recent data in a time-series DB keyed by meter and channel for per-meter queries; all history in a lakehouse partitioned by date and region for analytics; version values for corrections; compress with time-series encodings.

697
00:46:41.737 --> 00:46:43.017
Question 13.

698
00:46:43.297 --> 00:46:46.390
How do you detect consumers mapped to the wrong transformer?

699
00:46:46.670 --> 00:46:48.035
Thirty-second answer:

700
00:46:48.315 --> 00:47:04.742
Meters on the same transformer see correlated voltage fluctuations; compute correlations between each meter's voltage profile and its DT's (or neighbours') and flag meters that correlate better with another DT, then verify in the field and fix GIS.

701
00:47:06.092 --> 00:47:07.287
Question 14.

702
00:47:07.567 --> 00:47:10.148
How do you handle a storm with millions of events?

703
00:47:10.428 --> 00:47:11.793
Thirty-second answer:

704
00:47:12.073 --> 00:47:27.945
Kafka absorbs bursts, OMS correlation scales by region with topology-based grouping, commands get priority over reads, dashboards focus on feeder-level outages, pings confirm status, and consumer notifications are batched per outage.

705
00:47:29.295 --> 00:47:30.191
Architect.

706
00:47:30.471 --> 00:47:31.666
Question 15.

707
00:47:31.946 --> 00:47:36.170
Design an AMI platform for 25 million prepaid meters.

708
00:47:36.450 --> 00:47:37.815
Thirty-second answer:

709
00:47:38.095 --> 00:47:52.474
Mixed RF mesh and cellular network, HES with DLMS, HSM keys, scheduler and command manager; Kafka as the backbone; MDM with VEE, TSDB and lakehouse; a prepaid engine with ledger,

710
00:47:52.754 --> 00:48:05.213
policy-driven disconnects and fast reconnects; OMS with topology correlation; device management and FOTA; analytics for losses, theft and forecasting, with safety guardrails on commands.

711
00:48:06.563 --> 00:48:07.757
Question 16.

712
00:48:08.037 --> 00:48:12.048
What are the cybersecurity risks of smart metering and your mitigations?

713
00:48:12.328 --> 00:48:13.693
Thirty-second answer:

714
00:48:13.973 --> 00:48:33.579
Mass disconnect via a compromised HES, malicious firmware, key theft, spoofed data and privacy leaks; mitigate with segmentation, HSM per-meter keys, signed commands and firmware, rate limits and breakers, bulk approvals, OT security monitoring, and privacy controls on interval data.

715
00:48:34.929 --> 00:48:36.273
Question 17.

716
00:48:36.553 --> 00:48:39.198
How would you build energy accounting for a DISCOM?

717
00:48:39.478 --> 00:48:40.843
Thirty-second answer:

718
00:48:41.123 --> 00:48:59.214
Accurate topology (consumer, DT, feeder with effective dates), complete validated consumption, DT and feeder meters, daily balances with technical loss estimates, data quality checks (mapping via voltage correlation), and dashboards and work orders for high-loss areas.

719
00:49:00.564 --> 00:49:01.737
Question 18.

720
00:49:02.017 --> 00:49:07.052
How is IoT telemetry for meters different from generic IoT telemetry?

721
00:49:07.332 --> 00:49:08.697
Thirty-second answer:

722
00:49:08.977 --> 00:49:28.689
Billing-grade correctness and audit, regulated commands with physical consequences (disconnect), constrained networks, multi-vendor standardised protocols, long device lifetimes (10 plus years), and topology-aware analytics; generic IoT often favours best-effort metrics and MQTT.

723
00:49:30.039 --> 00:49:31.277
Question 19.

724
00:49:31.557 --> 00:49:33.562
How would you forecast load and use it?

725
00:49:33.842 --> 00:49:35.207
Thirty-second answer:

726
00:49:35.487 --> 00:49:51.637
Short-term forecasts per feeder and system from history, weather and calendar using gradient boosting or sequence models; use them for day-ahead power purchase, DT overload alerts, demand response baselines and planning for EVs and rooftop solar.

727
00:49:52.987 --> 00:49:54.096
Question 20.

728
00:49:54.376 --> 00:49:57.811
What happens to prepaid when the communication network fails?

729
00:49:58.091 --> 00:49:59.456
Thirty-second answer:

730
00:49:59.736 --> 00:50:16.163
Keep accepting recharges and crediting balances; pause disconnects (fail safe towards supply); queue reconnects with priority; estimate consumption from history and true up later; monitor communication health and dispatch field crews for persistent failures.

731
00:50:17.513 --> 00:50:18.558
Part 13.

732
00:50:18.838 --> 00:50:19.606
Summary.

733
00:50:20.856 --> 00:50:27.085
Smart metering replaces manual reads with 15-minute interval data, events and remote commands.

734
00:50:27.365 --> 00:50:33.147
It is the backbone for accurate billing, prepaid collections, outage detection and loss reduction.

735
00:50:33.427 --> 00:50:38.376
The HES handles communication (DLMS, scheduling, keys, commands).

736
00:50:38.656 --> 00:50:43.925
The MDM owns data truth (VEE, estimation, topology, aggregation).

737
00:50:44.205 --> 00:50:47.640
Kafka connects them to prepaid, the OMS and analytics.

738
00:50:47.920 --> 00:50:51.461
Prepaid is a central ledger with slab and TOD rating.

739
00:50:51.741 --> 00:50:56.115
Disconnects are policy-driven and rate-limited, and reconnects must be fast.

740
00:50:57.465 --> 00:51:05.785
Analytics turns data into action: energy accounting per DT, theft scoring, outage inference from topology, and forecasting.

741
00:51:06.065 --> 00:51:10.566
Safety and security matter more than usual because commands switch real power.

742
00:51:10.846 --> 00:51:14.921
Use circuit breakers, approvals, signed firmware and HSM keys.

